---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const path = '/how-tarot-works';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How Tarot Works — A Practical Explanation"
  description="How does tarot actually work? A grounded, non-mystical explanation of why tarot is a useful reflection tool."
  path={path}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How Tarot Works</h1>
    <p>
      Tarot doesn't predict the future. It works because you do. The cards are a mirror with structure: 78
      symbols that, when laid out in a specific shape, force you to look at a situation from a specific angle.
    </p>

    <h2>The mirror principle</h2>
    <p>
      When you draw a card, the image and its traditional meaning become a prompt. Your brain — which is
      extraordinarily good at finding patterns — does the rest. The card surfaces associations you already
      had but hadn't articulated.
    </p>

    <h2>The position is the meaning</h2>
    <p>
      The same card means different things in different positions. The Tower in "The Present" is a current
      crisis. The Tower in "The Outcome" is a coming release. The structure of the spread gives each card
      its precise message.
    </p>

    <h2>Upright and reversed</h2>
    <p>
      Reversed cards are not "bad." They are the same card turned inward — blocked, delayed, overdone, or
      asking to be looked at from the other side.
    </p>

    <h2>Why daily</h2>
    <p>
      A daily reading is a low-stakes way to keep the practice alive. One card, one moment, one prompt to
      notice. Over time, the cards become familiar and the meanings deepen.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
